#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:0
msgid ""
"Define and sign confdb schemas, then configure shared snap data through "
"views, plugs, hooks, and granular access controls."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:8
msgid "Configure snaps with confdb"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:10
msgid ""
"_Confdb_ is a configuration system that allows snaps to be configured in "
"fine-grained ways using a [confdb-schema](https://documentation.ubuntu.com/"
"core/reference/assertions/confdb-schema/) assertion."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:12
msgid ""
"This guide will detail the steps required to use confdb, as well as explore "
"some of its features. For implementation details, see the {ref}`Confdb "
"configuration mechanism <explanation-how-snaps-work-confdb-configuration-"
"mechanism>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:14
msgid ""
"We'll use an example where one snap creates a Wi-Fi configuration that "
"another snap can view."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:17
msgid ""
"Confdb is currently considered an {ref}`Experimental feature <ref-"
"experimental-features_experimental-features>` and implementation details may "
"change as development progresses."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:20
msgid "Prerequisites"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:22
msgid ""
"We first need to ensure the latest version of snapd is installed. This is "
"important as confdb is still being actively developed:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:24
msgid "snap refresh\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:28
msgid "We can then enable confdb with its experimental feature flag:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:30
msgid "sudo snap set system experimental.confdb=true\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:34
msgid "Creating a confdb-schema assertion"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:36
msgid ""
"To use confdb, we must first create a [confdb-schema assertion](https://"
"documentation.ubuntu.com/core/reference/assertions/confdb-schema/)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:38
msgid "There are two main parts to a confdb-schema assertion:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:39
#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:94
msgid "View rules"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:40
#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:48
msgid "Storage schema"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:42
msgid ""
"The *view rules* determine the “schema” of the configuration namespace "
"exposed to the snap, i.e., what paths can be accessed and how they’re mapped "
"onto the storage schema."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:44
msgid ""
"The *storage schema* determines what the actual stored data looks like and "
"what constraints apply."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:46
msgid "We’ll start by defining a storage schema."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:50
msgid ""
"When creating the storage schema, we can constrain the format and values "
"that the stored data can take, such as the characters you can include in a "
"password."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:52
msgid "For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:54
msgid ""
"  {\n"
"    \"storage\": {\n"
"      \"aliases\": {\n"
"        \"password\": {\n"
"          \"type\": \"string\",\n"
"          \"pattern\": \"^[\\w~!@#\\$%\\^&-\\+=\\;:,\\.\\/\\?]{8,63}$\"\n"
"        }\n"
"      },\n"
"      \"schema\": {\n"
"        \"v1\": {\n"
"          \"schema\": {\n"
"            \"wifi\": {\n"
"              \"values\": {\n"
"                \"schema\": {\n"
"                  \"psk\": \"${password}\",\n"
"                  \"ssid\": \"string\",\n"
"                  \"state\": {\n"
"                    \"type\": \"string\",\n"
"                    \"choices\": [\n"
"                      \"up\",\n"
"                      \"down\"\n"
"                    ]\n"
"                  }\n"
"                }\n"
"              }\n"
"            }\n"
"          }\n"
"        }\n"
"      }\n"
"    }\n"
"  }\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:88
msgid ""
"The above schema describes three fields within each Wi-Fi entry: an SSID, a "
"password and a connection state."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:90
msgid ""
"Snapd expects the storage schema to conform to a very precise format, using "
"2 spaces for indentation and sorting map entries. Both Python 3’s "
"`json.dump` and Golang’s `json.MarshalIndent` can be used to produce this "
"format. `jq -S` can also be used to sort the schema. It’s useful to keep the "
"storage schema in its own file so we can modify it over time."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:92
msgid ""
"See [confdb-schema types](https://documentation.ubuntu.com/core/reference/"
"assertions/confdb-schema/#schema-types) for a detailed description of types "
"and constraints."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:96
msgid "Now let’s create some view rules to access confdb."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:98
msgid ""
"In our example, we’ll use one snap as the custodian of both views, which "
"also allows changes to the network configuration, and another snap to access "
"it. This means we need two views: `wifi-admin` and `wifi-state`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:100
msgid "`wifi-admin` exposes parameters and allows them to be set."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:101
msgid ""
"`wifi-state` allows the snap to list Wi-Fi connections and read SSID and "
"state information (e.g., up, down)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:103
msgid "Both of these can be defined as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:105
msgid ""
"type:         confdb-schema\n"
"authority-id: <account-id>\n"
"account-id:   <account-id>\n"
"name:         network\n"
"summary:      Configure network parameters\n"
"timestamp:    2025-04-02T19:31:32Z\n"
"views:\n"
"  wifi-admin:\n"
"    summary: Configure Wi-Fi networks\n"
"    rules:\n"
"      -\n"
"        request: \"{name}.ssid\"\n"
"        storage: \"v1.wifi.{name}.ssid\"\n"
"      -\n"
"        request: \"{name}.password\"\n"
"        storage: \"v1.wifi.{name}.psk\"\n"
"      -\n"
"        request: \"{name}.state\"\n"
"        storage: \"v1.wifi.{name}.state\"\n"
"\n"
"  wifi-state:\n"
"    summary: List and read Wi-Fi SSIDs\n"
"    rules:\n"
"      -\n"
"        request: \"{name}\"\n"
"        storage: \"v1.wifi.{name}\"\n"
"        access: read\n"
"        content:\n"
"        -\n"
"          storage: ssid\n"
"        -\n"
"          storage: state\n"
"\n"
"body-length: 552\n"
"sign-key-sha3-384: 74KHeq1foV…\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:143
msgid ""
"See [Access Ubuntu One](https://documentation.ubuntu.com/core/tutorials/"
"build-your-first-image/access-ubuntu-one/#retrieve-your-developer-account-"
"id) for details on retrieving your `account-id`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:145
msgid "Putting it together"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:147
msgid ""
"Now we’re ready to create the assertion. Run the following command, filling "
"in the account ID and key name:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:149
msgid ""
"snapcraft edit-confdb-schema <account-id> network --key-name=<key-name>\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:153
msgid ""
"Next, edit the template assertion, replacing the views and storage schema "
"with the ones we defined above."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:155
msgid "The end result should look something like the following:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:157
msgid ""
"account-id: <account-id>\n"
"name: network\n"
"views:\n"
"  wifi-admin:\n"
"    rules:\n"
"      -\n"
"        request: \"{name}.ssid\"\n"
"        storage: \"v1.wifi.{name}.ssid\"\n"
"      -\n"
"        request: \"{name}.password\"\n"
"        storage: \"v1.wifi.{name}.psk\"\n"
"      -\n"
"        request: \"{name}.state\"\n"
"        storage: \"v1.wifi.{name}.state\"\n"
"\n"
"  wifi-state:\n"
"    rules:\n"
"      -\n"
"        request: \"{name}\"\n"
"        storage: \"v1.wifi.{name}\"\n"
"        access: read\n"
"        content:\n"
"          -\n"
"            storage: ssid\n"
"          -\n"
"            storage: state\n"
"\n"
"body: |-\n"
"  {\n"
"    \"storage\": {\n"
"      \"aliases\": {\n"
"        \"password\": {\n"
"          \"pattern\": \"^[a-zA-Z0-9~!@#\\\\$%&-=:,\\\\.]{8,63}$\",\n"
"          \"type\": \"string\"\n"
"        }\n"
"      },\n"
"      \"schema\": {\n"
"        \"v1\": {\n"
"          \"schema\": {\n"
"            \"wifi\": {\n"
"              \"values\": {\n"
"                \"schema\": {\n"
"                  \"psk\": \"${password}\",\n"
"                  \"ssid\": \"string\",\n"
"                  \"state\": {\n"
"                    \"choices\": [\"up\", \"down\"],\n"
"                    \"type\": \"string\"\n"
"                  }\n"
"                }\n"
"              }\n"
"            }\n"
"          }\n"
"        }\n"
"      }\n"
"    }\n"
"  }\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:216
msgid "Then exit the editor and sign the assertion when prompted."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:218
msgid "Creating the snaps"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:220
msgid ""
"See [Create a new snap](https://documentation.ubuntu.com/snapcraft/stable/"
"tutorials/craft-a-snap/) for a general overview of the snap creation process."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:222
msgid "There are two things a snap needs to be able to use confdb:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:223
msgid "a plug to declare its intent to use a confdb namespace and"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:224
msgid ""
"an optional set of {ref}`hooks <reference-development-supported-snap-hooks>` "
"that snapd invokes when the namespace is accessed"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:226
msgid "Custodian snap"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:228
msgid ""
"The interface plug specifies an `account` and a `view` which together "
"identify the view through which the configuration is being accessed. It can "
"also optionally declare a `role`, with a value of `custodian`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:230
msgid ""
"Custodian snaps have a special role when accessing ephemeral data. They're "
"responsible for saving or loading this data to an external source outside of "
"snapd."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:232
msgid ""
"The data that snapd stores for ephemeral configuration is only a cached, non-"
"authoritative version of the external data. At least one custodian snap must "
"be installed and connected for each non-system confdb view."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:234
msgid ""
"The first snap will act as the custodian for both views as well as allowing "
"configuration of the Wi-Fi network, so let’s configure it accordingly:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:236
msgid ""
"plugs:\n"
"  network-wifi-admin:\n"
"    interface: confdb\n"
"    account: <account-id>\n"
"    view: network/wifi-admin\n"
"    role: custodian\n"
"  network-wifi-state:\n"
"    interface: confdb\n"
"    account: <account-id>\n"
"    view: network/wifi-state\n"
"    role: custodian\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:250
msgid ""
"The other component that must be defined in the snap are the {ref}`hooks "
"<reference-development-supported-snap-hooks>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:252
msgid ""
"Snaps interact with confdb through {ref}`snapctl <how-to-guides-manage-snaps-"
"use-snapctl>` and, in turn, snapd invokes various hooks when confdb is read "
"from or written to."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:254
msgid ""
"In this case, the custodian snap will only define the `change-view-<plug>`, "
"which provides an opportunity for the snap to modify values being set."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:256
msgid ""
"As an example, we’ll say that the SSID must be prefixed with our company’s "
"name: “Acme”. The configuration can be set by the administrator using {ref}"
"`snap set <how-to-guides-work-with-snaps-configure-snaps>` or by any other "
"snap with access to that view, so it’s the custodian snap’s responsibility "
"to enforce the prefix."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:258
msgid "Our `change-view-network-wifi-admin` hook looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:260
msgid ""
"#!/bin/bash -x\n"
"\n"
"# prefix the SSID with \"acme\", if not already present\n"
"value=$(snapctl get --view :network-wifi-admin acme.ssid)\n"
"if [[ \"$?\" -eq 0 && \"$value\" != acme-* ]]; then\n"
"  snapctl set --view :network-wifi-admin acme.ssid=\"acme-$value\"\n"
"fi\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:270
msgid "That’s it for the custodian snap."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:272
msgid "Reader snap"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:274
msgid ""
"Now we’ll create a snap that will only read the configuration. Its plug will "
"reference the read-only view and it will omit the `role`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:276
msgid ""
"plugs:\n"
"  network-wifi-state:\n"
"    interface: confdb\n"
"    account: <account-id>\n"
"    view: network/wifi-state\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:284
msgid ""
"Non-custodian snaps can only define an `observe-view-<plug>` hook, which "
"allows them to be notified when the confdb-schema referenced by that plug "
"has been used to modify data."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:286
msgid ""
"#!/bin/sh -xe\n"
"\n"
"# read the new SSID and store it somewhere\n"
"new_ssid=$(snapctl get --view :network-wifi-state acme.ssid)\n"
"echo \"$new_ssid\" >> \"$SNAP_COMMON\"/ssid\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:294
msgid "Install and connect the snaps"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:296
msgid ""
"We can mediate snap access to confdb-schema views by connecting their confdb "
"plugs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:298
msgid ""
"Note that when a snap is published by the same account ID as the assertion, "
"the interface plug will be auto-connected."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:300
msgid ""
"snap install custodian-snap reader-snap\n"
"snap connect custodian-snap:network-wifi-admin\n"
"snap connect custodian-snap:network-wifi-state\n"
"snap connect reader-snap:network-wifi-state\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:307
msgid "Setting data"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:309
msgid ""
"Now we’re ready to start setting data, we’ll use `snap run --shell` to mimic "
"the snaps interacting with confdb:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:311
msgid ""
"$ sudo snap run --shell custodian-snap\n"
"# snapctl set --view :network-wifi-admin acme.ssid=some-ssid "
"acme.password=super-secret\n"
"# exit\n"
"\n"
"$ sudo snap run --shell reader-snap\n"
"# snapctl get --view :network-wifi-state acme.ssid\n"
"acme-some-ssid\n"
"# exit\n"
"\n"
"$ snap get <account-id>/network/wifi-state acme.ssid\n"
"acme-some-ssid\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:325
msgid ""
"As expected, the `change-view-network-wifi-admin` hook was invoked when "
"`custodian-snap` modified the SSID and prefixed the value with \"acme\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:327
msgid ""
"We were also able to read the same value from both another connected snap "
"and through the snapd API using `snap get`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:329
msgid ""
"We can also check that the `reader-snap`'s `observe-view-network-wifi-state` "
"hook was invoked when the SSID was changed and that it saved the new value "
"in SNAP\\_COMMON:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:331
msgid ""
"$ snap changes\n"
"ID   Status  Spawn               Ready               Summary\n"
"123  Done    today at ...        today at ...        Set confdb through "
"\"<account-id>/network/wifi-admin\"\n"
"...\n"
"\n"
"$ snap change 123\n"
"Status  Spawn  Ready  Summary\n"
"Done    ...    ...    Clears the ongoing confdb transaction from state (on "
"error)\n"
"Done    ...    ...    Run hook change-view-network-wifi-admin of snap "
"\"custodian-snap\"\n"
"Done    ...    ...    Commit changes to confdb (<account-id>/network/wifi-"
"admin)\n"
"Done    ...    ...    Run hook observe-view-network-wifi-state of snap "
"\"reader-snap\"\n"
"Done    ...    ...    Clears the ongoing confdb transaction from state\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"$ cat /snap/reader-snap/common/ssid\n"
"acme-some-ssid\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:350
msgid ""
"Note that concurrent accesses may block if they would result in writes "
"running concurrently with another access. If any access is blocked, "
"subsequent accesses will also be queued to ensure that they are run in order."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:352
msgid ""
"For example, if there is a read ongoing and a write is requested, the write "
"will be blocked. A new read would then also be queued and only run after the "
"write, even though reads are normally run concurrently. This ensures that a "
"stream of reads cannot starve a write access."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:354
msgid "Default values"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:356
msgid ""
"Confdb has no schema-level mechanism for declaring default values, but both "
"`snap` and `snapctl` allow supplying a fallback with `--default`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:358
msgid ""
"$ snap get --default=acme-default <account-id>/network/wifi-state acme.ssid\n"
"acme-default\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:363
msgid ""
"In both cases, `--default` can only be used when requesting a single path."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:365
msgid "Getting secret data"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:366
msgid "*From snapd version 2.76+*"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:368
msgid ""
"If we wanted to ensure that only admins were able to access certain data in "
"confdb storage, we could add a visibility field to storage entries. For "
"example, say we wanted only admins to read the `psk` field defined earlier. "
"To limit access to `psk`, in the storage schema definition, we would change"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:370
msgid "\"psk\": \"${password}\",\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:374
msgid "to"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/configure-snaps-with-confdb.md:376
msgid ""
"\"psk\": {\n"
"  \"type\": \"${password}\",\n"
"  \"visibility\": \"secret\"\n"
"},\n"
msgstr ""

